---

<Base title={title} description={description} canonical="https://sustainabilitydiet.com/one-hundred-camels/critics" readingBar>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16">

    <p class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">
      <a href="/one-hundred-camels" class="hover:text-ochre-deep">One Hundred Camels</a>
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-display text-5xl md:text-6xl tracking-tight leading-[1.02] mb-6">
      Critics
    </h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-ink/75 leading-relaxed font-serif">
      The strongest arguments against Pleistocene rewilding, set out on their own terms. If the proposal on this site cannot withstand these objections, it is not worth running.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 small-caps text-xs text-ink/60 tracking-wide">
      Each critic steel-manned first, then answered. Not dismissed.
    </div>

    {/* Jump-nav */}
    <nav class="mt-10 border-l-2 border-ink/15 pl-5 print:hidden">
      <p class="small-caps text-[0.65rem] text-ochre-deep tracking-wide mb-2">Jump to</p>
      <ol class="space-y-1">
        <li><a href="#caro" class="font-serif text-ink/80 hover:text-ink hover:underline decoration-ochre underline-offset-4"><span class="small-caps text-[0.65rem] text-ochre-deep tabular-nums mr-2">01</span>The proxies are not the same species <span class="text-ink/55">— Caro 2007</span></a></li>
        <li><a href="#rubenstein" class="font-serif text-ink/80 hover:text-ink hover:underline decoration-ochre underline-offset-4"><span class="small-caps text-[0.65rem] text-ochre-deep tabular-nums mr-2">02</span>The risks are irreversible and asymmetric <span class="text-ink/55">— Rubenstein et al. 2006</span></a></li>
        <li><a href="#oliveira-santos" class="font-serif text-ink/80 hover:text-ink hover:underline decoration-ochre underline-offset-4"><span class="small-caps text-[0.65rem] text-ochre-deep tabular-nums mr-2">03</span>This is ecological colonialism <span class="text-ink/55">— Oliveira-Santos &amp; Fernandez 2010</span></a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-the-critics-get-right" class="font-serif text-ink/80 hover:text-ink hover:underline decoration-ochre underline-offset-4"><span class="small-caps text-[0.65rem] text-ochre-deep tabular-nums mr-2">04</span>What the critics get right that we should not lose</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <hr class="hairline my-12" />

    <div class="prose-reading max-w-none font-serif text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 space-y-10">

      <p>
        The original 2005 Donlan et al. proposal in <em>Nature</em> &mdash; "Re-wilding North America" &mdash; was a flare. It was meant to provoke, and it did. The responses that followed over the next decade constitute the actual literature on Pleistocene rewilding. Three of them are load-bearing.
      </p>
      <p>
        The standard of this page is that a reader should come away able to summarize each critic's argument in their own voice before seeing the response. If a critic's position here sounds weak, I have failed the exercise and should rewrite it.
      </p>

    </div>

    <hr class="hairline my-16" />

    <!-- Critic 1: Caro 2007 -->
    <section id="caro" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
      <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">01</div>
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl tracking-tight leading-tight mb-4">
        The proxies are not the same species.
      </h2>
      <p class="font-serif text-base text-ink/60 italic mb-8">
        Caro, T. (2007). The Pleistocene re-wilding gambit. <em>Trends in Ecology &amp; Evolution</em>, 22(6), 281&ndash;283.
      </p>

      <div class="prose-reading max-w-none font-serif text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 space-y-5">
        <p>
          Caro's central objection is not about risk. It is about category. He argues that the Pleistocene rewilding proposal relies on a slippage between <em>taxonomic</em> substitution (a living species that is phylogenetically close to the extinct one) and <em>functional</em> substitution (a living species that does the same ecological work), and then quietly treats the two as interchangeable.
        </p>
        <p>
          They are not. An African cheetah is not <em>Miracinonyx trumani</em>. A Bactrian camel is not <em>Camelops hesternus</em>. The cheetah did not evolve chasing pronghorns on the North American plains; the pronghorn's extraordinary speed is the fossilized evidence of a predator that is no longer there, and which a different-continent cheetah will not duplicate just by being fast and spotted. To call this "restoration" is to smuggle an assumption about function that the paleontological evidence does not support.
        </p>
        <p>
          Caro's second move is about scale. The Donlan proposal suggested releasing large carnivores on private reserves in the Great Plains. Caro points out that the operational machinery &mdash; fencing, staffing, legal liability, public acceptance &mdash; is orders of magnitude beyond what the conservation community has ever built in North America. Even if the ecology is right, the implementation is a fantasy.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose my-10 p-6 bg-bone/30 border-l-2 border-ochre">
          <p class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3 tracking-wide">Response</p>
          <div class="font-serif text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 space-y-4">
            <p>
              Caro is right about the category slippage. A project that describes itself as "restoring" an extinct species is overclaiming. This site deliberately does not use "restoration" for the camel experiment. The proposed work is explicitly called a <em>proxy substitution</em>, with the acknowledgment that the extinct species and the living one are not the same, and that the goal is to test whether the <em>function</em> can return, not the species. That is a narrower claim than Donlan 2005 made and it is the one the experiment is trying to test.
            </p>
            <p>
              Caro is also right about scale. The scale of the proposal on this site is deliberately smaller than Donlan's: one hundred thousand acres, one species (Bactrian camel), no predator release, no attempt to network multiple reserves into a continental ecology. The experiment is a unit, not a program. If it fails, it fails on one site. If it works, the question of scale is the next question, not this one.
            </p>
            <p>
              What the critic's frame rules out &mdash; large apex-predator release, continental-scale program design &mdash; is not what is being proposed here. The remaining live question is whether a <em>functional</em> proxy, at a sub-continental scale, can produce measurable changes in fruit dispersal, soil, and plant community composition. On that narrower claim, Caro's objection does not land as forcefully.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Critic 2: Rubenstein -->
    <section id="rubenstein" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
      <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">02</div>
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl tracking-tight leading-tight mb-4">
        The risks are irreversible and asymmetric.
      </h2>
      <p class="font-serif text-base text-ink/60 italic mb-8">
        Rubenstein, D. R., Rubenstein, D. I., Sherman, P. W., &amp; Gavin, T. A. (2006). Pleistocene park: Does re-wilding North America represent sound conservation for the 21st century? <em>Biological Conservation</em>, 132(2), 232&ndash;238.
      </p>

      <div class="prose-reading max-w-none font-serif text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 space-y-5">
        <p>
          Rubenstein and colleagues grant, for the sake of argument, that the functional-restoration logic holds. Their concern is what happens when the introduction goes wrong &mdash; and their point is that the consequences of the failure modes are asymmetric with the benefits of the success.
        </p>
        <p>
          A successful introduction produces gradual, diffuse ecological change that may take a decade to detect. A failed introduction can produce an invasive-species cascade, pathogen transmission to naïve native fauna, or disease outbreak in the introduced population itself. Those failure modes can be fast and effectively irreversible at ecosystem scale. The critique is not "this is too risky" in the blanket sense. It is "the distribution of outcomes is skewed such that a small probability of catastrophic failure outweighs the expected value of success."
        </p>
        <p>
          They are especially concerned about predators, about pathogens, and about hybridization with extant populations. A cheetah released in North America is not going to hybridize with anything, but a camel released near a small feral dromedary population in the Southwest might.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose my-10 p-6 bg-bone/30 border-l-2 border-ochre">
          <p class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3 tracking-wide">Response</p>
          <div class="font-serif text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 space-y-4">
            <p>
              This is the objection that shapes the experiment's design. Every pre-registered failure criterion on the <a href="/one-hundred-camels/the-experiment#failure" class="text-ochre-deep">experiment page</a> is a direct response to a Rubenstein-class concern: quarantine-and-screen protocol for introduction, veterinary monitoring at regular intervals, plant-community disturbance thresholds that trigger removal, and a termination procedure that is funded and staffed from day one.
            </p>
            <p>
              Asymmetry is a real feature of this class of intervention and the answer cannot be "we will be careful." The answer has to be: the experiment is stopped early if any of the named failure criteria trigger, the population does not expand until year five, and the site is chosen specifically to minimize hybridization and pathogen-transfer risk. A site far from feral dromedary populations. A site with controllable fencing. A committee that retains removal authority.
            </p>
            <p>
              Rubenstein's critique does not invalidate the experiment. It rewrites the protocol. On the version of the protocol this site is proposing, the Rubenstein frame is not an objection &mdash; it is the shape of the safeguards.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Critic 3: Oliveira-Santos & Fernandez -->
    <section id="oliveira-santos" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
      <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">03</div>
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl tracking-tight leading-tight mb-4">
        This is ecological colonialism.
      </h2>
      <p class="font-serif text-base text-ink/60 italic mb-8">
        Oliveira-Santos, L. G. R., &amp; Fernandez, F. A. S. (2010). Pleistocene rewilding, frankenstein ecosystems, and an alternative conservation agenda. <em>Conservation Biology</em>, 24(1), 4&ndash;5. Also restated across several subsequent papers and in Indigenous-led critiques of the broader rewilding discourse.
      </p>

      <div class="prose-reading max-w-none font-serif text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 space-y-5">
        <p>
          This is the quietest of the three critiques and arguably the strongest. It begins by noting who gets to decide what an ecosystem "should" look like. Pleistocene rewilding positions a specific baseline &mdash; pre-Clovis, roughly thirteen thousand years ago &mdash; as the reference ecology. That baseline is before humans arrived. Choosing it is choosing an ecology without humans in it.
        </p>
        <p>
          North America was not empty between the terminal-Pleistocene extinctions and European contact. Indigenous peoples shaped the continent for ten thousand years through burning regimes, selective forestry, bison management, and seed curation. The ecology that existed on October 11, 1492 was not a baseline; it was a negotiation.
        </p>
        <p>
          To propose that the right reference ecology is the one from before human arrival, rather than the one Indigenous people actively maintained, is to erase that management as ecologically uninteresting &mdash; a strong and colonial claim, even when it is made for good conservation reasons. The critique is not about whether camels should walk a fence line in Texas. It is about who gets to decide which absences matter and which presences matter, and on whose timeline.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose my-10 p-6 bg-bone/30 border-l-2 border-ochre">
          <p class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3 tracking-wide">Response</p>
          <div class="font-serif text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 space-y-4">
            <p>
              This critique is the one this project takes most seriously. It is also the one with the fewest easy answers.
            </p>
            <p>
              The honest response is that the proposal here is not trying to restore a pre-human baseline. It is trying to restore one specific ecological function &mdash; the dispersal of fruits into mouths that could handle them &mdash; that has been missing for thirteen thousand years and was missing during the Indigenous stewardship era as well. Native peoples lived with the extinction of <em>Camelops</em> for ten thousand years before Europeans arrived. Their management systems adapted to that absence.
            </p>
            <p>
              The experiment is not a claim that the Indigenous era was ecologically insufficient. It is a claim that a specific ecological function has been vacant since before either Indigenous or European stewardship, and that testing whether a proxy can re-establish it is worth doing on land owned and controlled by people who want to try.
            </p>
            <p>
              The experiment is, to the extent possible, committed to seeking consultation with Tribal Nations whose traditional territories include proposed sites, before any introduction. Not as an endorsement requirement &mdash; that would be condescending &mdash; but because the local knowledge about what that ground was, what was on it, and what has changed is often better than what is in the scientific record. The committee structure described on the experiment page is designed to accommodate this.
            </p>
            <p>
              This does not answer the critique in full. It cannot. The deeper version &mdash; that choosing a pre-Clovis baseline is a colonial move even when done with care &mdash; is a philosophical position that the proposal does not fully resolve. The honest answer is: <em>we are choosing to test one function; we are not proposing a continental program; we are not claiming a universal baseline; we are working with people who own the land and want the test; and we will stop if the work is doing more harm than it is learning from</em>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Epilogue -->
    <section id="what-the-critics-get-right" class="mt-20 pt-12 border-t-2 border-ink/20 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl tracking-tight mb-8">
        What the critics get right that we should not lose.
      </h2>
      <div class="prose-reading max-w-none font-serif text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 space-y-5">
        <p>
          1. <strong>Proxies are not the extinct species.</strong> The project must never describe itself as "bringing back" <em>Camelops</em>. It is testing whether a camel can do camel-shaped work on camel-shaped ground. That is a smaller claim, and it has to stay smaller.
        </p>
        <p>
          2. <strong>The failure modes are asymmetric.</strong> A conservative protocol with funded termination is not paranoid. It is the only ethical version of the proposal. If the committee that holds back does not hold back, the work is not worth doing.
        </p>
        <p>
          3. <strong>The baseline question is not settled.</strong> There is no neutral reference ecology. Choosing pre-Clovis is a choice with consequences. The experiment needs to say so out loud, every time, and work with the people whose ongoing relationship to the land gives them a different and equally load-bearing claim on what restoration means.
        </p>
        <p>
          4. <strong>The field is small.</strong> Rewilding as a discipline is roughly twenty years old. The peer community is narrow. Any proposal that does not submit itself to that community for pre-registration, scrutiny, and ongoing review is not a scientific experiment &mdash; it is a stunt. The experiment page names its committee structure for that reason.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-ink/10 flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
      <a
